## API Compatibility Bugs (from doc/api-compatibility.md assessment)

### response-status-is-method-not-property (lib/lws/response.js, WHATWG Fetch
  spec): Response.status is implemented as a chainable setter method
  (status(code)) with a separate statusCode getter, instead of the spec's
  readonly unsigned short property. Code that does response.status === 200
  compares a function reference to a number and always evaluates to false.
  This is the single most serious spec violation in the Fetch implementation
  and breaks all standard Response handling code. The method form exists
  because the protocol layer patches status onto Response objects at runtime
  for client responses, but the design should have been a property from the
  start. Fix: convert status to a property, keep statusCode as deprecated alias.

    const res = new Response('body', { status: 200 });
    console.log(res.status === 200); // false - res.status is a function
    console.log(res.statusCode === 200); // true - but non-standard name

### body-formData-returns-wrong-type (lib/lws/body.js, WHATWG Fetch spec):
  Body.formData() returns a plain null-prototype object instead of a
  FormData instance. No FormData class exists in the codebase at all.
  Code that does instanceof FormData or calls .get()/.getAll()/.append()
  on the result will fail. The spec mandates Promise<FormData>.
  Fix: implement a FormData class, update formData() to return an instance.

    const form = await response.formData();
    console.log(form instanceof FormData); // false - plain object
    console.log(form.get('field')); // TypeError - no get() method

### headers-iteration-order-wrong (lib/lws/headers.js, WHATWG Fetch spec):
  Headers iterates in insertion order (plain object for...in) instead of
  the spec-mandated sorted (lexicographic by lowercase name) order.
  Code that depends on sorted header ordering produces different results
  than spec-compliant implementations. Fix: sort headers by name before
  iteration in keys()/values()/entries()/[Symbol.iterator]().

    const h = new Headers();
    h.set('z-header', '1');
    h.set('a-header', '2');
    for (const [name] of h) console.log(name);
    // prints: z-header, a-header (insertion order)
    // spec requires: a-header, z-header (sorted order)

### fetch-no-request-input (lib/fetch.js, WHATWG Fetch spec): fetch() only
  accepts URL strings as the first argument. The spec allows fetch(input)
  where input can be a Request object or a URL string. Code that constructs
  Request objects and passes them to fetch() will fail. Fix: check if first
  argument is a Request instance and extract url/method/headers/body from it.

    const req = new Request('https://example.com/', { method: 'POST', body: 'data' });
    const res = await fetch(req); // fails - expects URL string

### fetch-wrong-error-type (lib/fetch.js, WHATWG Fetch spec): Network errors
  throw a custom ConnectionError class instead of the spec-mandated TypeError.
  Error handling code that catches TypeError for network failures won't catch
  qjs-lws errors. Fix: throw TypeError (or a subclass) instead of ConnectionError.

    try { await fetch('https://nonexistent.invalid/'); }
    catch(e) { console.log(e instanceof TypeError); } // false - ConnectionError

### fetch-abortsignal-incomplete (lib/fetch.js, WHATWG Fetch spec): Multiple
  problems with AbortSignal handling: (1) doesn't throw DOMException with
  name "AbortError" when aborted, (2) overwrites existing signal.onabort
  handler instead of using addEventListener, (3) no pre-check if signal is
  already aborted before making request. Code that catches AbortError won't
  work, existing signal handlers get overwritten, pre-aborted signals don't
  prevent the request. Fix: throw error with name='AbortError', use
  addEventListener, check signal.aborted before initiating.

    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    ctrl.abort();
    try { await fetch('https://example.com/', { signal: ctrl.signal }); }
    catch(e) { console.log(e.name === 'AbortError'); } // false - wrong error type

### missing-bytes-method (lib/lws/body.js, lib/lws/response.js, WHATWG Fetch
  spec): Neither Body nor Response implements the bytes() method that returns
  Promise<Uint8Array>. This is a newer addition to the Fetch spec but is
  straightforward to implement (similar to arrayBuffer() but returns Uint8Array).

### clone-no-bodyused-check (lib/lws/request.js, lib/lws/response.js, WHATWG
  Fetch spec): Request.clone() and Response.clone() don't check if bodyUsed
  is true before cloning. The spec requires clone() to throw TypeError if the
  body has already been consumed. Cloning a consumed request/response doesn't
  throw as expected. Fix: add if (this.bodyUsed) throw new TypeError(...) at
  start of clone() methods.

### websocket-missing-url-property (lib/websocket.js, WHATWG WebSocket API):
  The WebSocket constructor receives a URL but doesn't store it, so
  websocket.url returns undefined. The spec defines a readonly url property.
  Fix: store URL in constructor and expose as getter.

    const ws = new WebSocket('wss://echo.websocket.events/');
    console.log(ws.url); // undefined - should be 'wss://echo.websocket.events/'

### server-missing-url-property (lib/serve.js, Bun.js Server API): Bun.js
  provides server.url as a URL object (e.g., http://localhost:3000) but the
  implementation only stores port and hostname separately. Bun.js code that
  uses server.url won't work. Fix: add url getter that constructs URL from
  hostname and port.

    const server = serve({ port: 3000 }, req => new Response('ok'));
    console.log(server.url); // undefined - should be URL { href: 'http://localhost:3000/' }

### eventtarget-missing-options (lib/lws/events.js, WHATWG DOM API):
  addEventListener() doesn't support the options parameter (capture, once,
  passive, signal). Can't use { once: true } for one-time listeners or
  { signal } for AbortSignal-based listener removal. The minimal EventTarget
  is sufficient for internal use but lacks features useful for user code.
  Fix: add options parameter, prioritize once and signal.

### udpsocket-send-different-signature (lib/udpsocket.js, Bun.js UDP API):
  Bun uses socket.send(data, port, address) but the implementation uses
  socket.sendTo(data, peer) where peer is a sockaddr object. Bun.js UDP code
  won't work without adaptation. Fix: add send(data, port, address) method
  matching Bun's signature, keep sendTo() as alternative.

### server-missing-11-methods (lib/serve.js, Bun.js Server API): Server
  object is missing 11 of 15 properties/methods: url (see above), reload()
  for hot-reload, ref()/unref() for process lifecycle, subscriberCount(),
  requestIP(), timeout(), pendingRequests, pendingWebSockets,
  closeIdleConnections(), development flag, id, fetch() for internal requests.
  Impact: many Bun.js server management patterns won't work (graceful shutdown,
  health checks, metrics).

### websockethandler-missing-10-options (lib/serve.js, Bun.js WebSocket API):
  WebSocketHandler is missing 10 of 13 options: drain() for backpressure,
  ping()/pong() handlers, perMessageDeflate compression, maxPayloadLength,
  idleTimeout, backpressureLimit, closeOnBackpressureLimit, sendPings,
  publishToSelf. Can't implement backpressure handling, compression, or
  WebSocket lifecycle management.

### serverwebsocket-missing-3-members (lib/websocket.js, Bun.js WebSocket API):
  ServerWebSocket missing remoteAddress (client address getter), subscriptions
  (array of subscribed topics), cork() (batch writes). Can't access client IP
  from WebSocket handlers or inspect subscription state.

### udpsocket-missing-10-methods (lib/udpsocket.js, Bun.js UDP API):
  UDPSocket missing sendMany() for batch send, setBroadcast(), setTTL(),
  addMembership()/dropMembership() for multicast, setMulticastTTL(),
  setMulticastLoopback(), setMulticastInterface(),
  addSourceSpecificMembership()/dropSourceSpecificMembership() for SSM.
  Can't implement multicast, broadcast, or socket options.

### response-cookie-clearCookie-are-express-not-whatwg (lib/lws/response.js):
  Response.cookie() and Response.clearCookie() are Express-style convenience
  methods that don't belong on the WHATWG Response class. They return `this`
  for chaining (non-standard) and are only meaningful for server-side
  responses. These should be moved to ServerResponse only.

    const res = new Response('body');
    res.cookie('session', 'abc123');  // works but shouldn't - not WHATWG
    // Should only be available on ServerResponse:
    app.get('/', (req, res) => res.cookie('session', 'abc123').end('ok'));

---

See doc/api-compatibility.md for full compatibility assessment with code examples and priority ranking.

